    "body": "Aerial view of a large-scale nickel mining site on a tropical island near Wayag, Raja Ampat, Indonesia. ( Image by Tom | AdobeStock. )\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 25, 2026\nA sulphur shortage threatens projects that would add more than 600,000 tonnes of nickel capacity in Indonesia ’s battery-materials sector, Wood Mackenzie said Wednesday.\nIndonesia had relied on the Middle East for about 80% of its sulphur imports before the closure of the Strait of Hormuz. Sulphur imports fell 29% year-over-year in the first half of 2026 and average prices nearly tripled, the consultancy reported .\n“Sulphur supply constraints are unlikely to ease quickly,” Wood Mackenzie research associate Ziyi Liu said. “Logistics normalisation takes time even if conflict-related disruptions subside, and persistent competition from the fertiliser sector limits the reallocation of available supply to industrial buyers.”\nIndonesian plants use sulphur to make the acid that extracts nickel and cobalt from ore for use in batteries. Their high-pressure acid leach process produces mixed hydroxide precipitate, an intermediate used to make battery chemicals.\nSulphur accounts for roughly half the operating cost of these plants, the International Energy Agency estimates. Indonesia, the world’s biggest nickel producer, mined an estimated 2.6 million tonnes in 2025, according to the United States Geological Survey . That represents about two-thirds of world output.\nProduction cuts\nDrought has also hit operating plants. Smelters at the Indonesia Morowali Industrial Park on Sulawesi reported production cuts of 30% to 45% in late September as water grew scarce and barges struggled to deliver coal, Wood Mackenzie said.\nThe squeeze extends to stainless steel. Falling grades in Indonesian ore have cut production of nickel pig iron, a lower-grade nickel product, prompting stainless mills to buy more pure nickel and use more scrap.\nScrap now makes up about a quarter of the stainless-steel feed mix, up from 21% to 22% a few years ago, Wood Mackenzie estimates.\nEven with mills buying more pure nickel, Wood Mackenzie expects a surplus of high-purity metal to persist unless supply also falls. It forecasts that global consumption of refined cobalt will decline this year. The London Metal Exchange cash nickel price settled at $15,060 (C$21,530) per tonne Tuesday.\nPrint\nTAG: battery metals Indonesia Nickel\nCOMMODITY: Nickel\nREGION: Asia\nRepublish this article",
